 "/2012/09/24/building-a-home-automation-system-the-broker-and-sensors-part-2/"
 [  {:name "TheBadger" :created "2013-01-25 00:19" :body "<p>Hey, I am very interested in how this works and just wondered if you could explain a bit more of the hardware..</p><p>as I see it, your Pi is listening on Port 8888 for data.. you have a jeenode which looks as if it is connected to the serial port on the pi?</p><p>So are you listening on the serial port on the pi and then sending serial data to port 8888 which this other program picks up?</p><p>I would like to how I get from a temperature reading in my micro which I have formatted to JSON to the actual TCP stuff on the Pi?</p>" :children [ {:name "Stian Eikeland" :created "2013-01-25 22:05" :body "<p>Hello :)<br>So, there are a few steps to this and I'll try to explain.</p><p>On the hardware side there is a jeenode connected to uart on the raspberry pi.<br>(Also PWR is connected to 5v out on raspberry pi, and ground to ground).<br>Have a look at: <a href=\"http://elinux.org/RPi_Low-level_peripherals\" rel=\"nofollow\">http://elinux.org/RPi_Low-l...</a><br>GPIO14 and 15 double as TX and RX pins.</p><p>There are some more steps you need to do to get this working, as the PI<br>has a terminal running on serial per default, see here how to disable it:<br><a href=\"http://www.irrational.net/2012/04/19/using-the-raspberry-pis-serial-port/\" rel=\"nofollow\">http://www.irrational.net/2...</a></p><p>If you now configure the jeenode/microcontroller to transmit readings via<br>serial/uart and open the serial device on the PI you should be able to read them.<br>You can use screen to manually read this (for speed 9600 baud): screen /dev/ttyAMA0 9600</p><p>I then have a node/coffeescript program that reads this and relays it to a \"message broker\"<br>via ZeroMQ. <a href=\"https://github.com/stianeikeland/homeautomation/blob/master/services/mcu-relay/mcu-relay.coffee\" rel=\"nofollow\">https://github.com/stianeik...</a><br>There is a few other things going on in the script as the microcontroller also controls my home cinema receiver.</p><p>The \"message broker\" <a href=\"https://github.com/stianeikeland/homeautomation/blob/master/broker/broker.coffee\" rel=\"nofollow\">https://github.com/stianeik...</a><br>then relays these messages to all other services that subscribe to them.</p><p>I use ZeroMQ ( <a href=\"http://www.zeromq.org/\" rel=\"nofollow\">http://www.zeromq.org/</a> ) for this. ZeroMQ is kinda an abstraction layer on top of TCP/IP<br>that give you message queue like functionality, it also takes away all the boring stuff of networking<br>(handling disconnects, reconnects, packages, etc..). In retrospect I think I would have been<br>better served using Redis, as it would have made it easier to keep global state.</p><p>Hope this is of any help :)</p>" :children []}]}
 ]
