 ]
 "/2014/10/06/pid-transducer/"
 [  {:name "Alex Miller" :created "2014-10-06 15:39" :body "<p>Nicely done! :)  One subtle but important point - \"stateful transducer\" is not actually correct as the transducer itself is just functions. Rather, the reducing function *created by* the transducer is stateful. So each time the transducer is *applied*, new state is created.</p><p>Also, volatiles *are* thread-safe (reads are guaranteed to see prior writes) and read and write are atomic operations. What they lack is the ability to create a compound atomic swap! operation - volatiles rely on thread isolation (encapsulated in the transducing process) to make the compound operation safe in practice.</p>" :children [ {:name "Stian Eikeland" :created "2014-10-06 15:48" :body "<p>Thank you, and thanks for the corrections/clarifications. I've updated the post! (might take a couple of minutes before the changes show because of CDN).</p>" :children []}]}
  {:name "mplewis" :created "2014-10-06 17:58" :body "<p>Awesome writeup! How did you find the initial PID values?</p><p>I built something sorta-similar but with just bang-bang control: <a href=\"https://github.com/mplewis/bean-sous-vide\" rel=\"nofollow\">https://github.com/mplewis/...</a></p>" :children [ {:name "Stian Eikeland" :created "2014-10-06 18:12" :body "<p>Thank you! The parameters are pure guesswork, I did a couple of runs with just water and adjusted them as needed. They aren't very good as the kettle overshot pretty heavily before stabilising. This is only a toy setup for one evening, so removing overshooting and small oscillations wasn't that important.</p><p>That's a pretty cool setup you have there btw :)</p><p>For my real sous-vide setup I've tuned it using the Cohen-Coon method. I've also found that direct elements (tea heater) is much easier than my slow responding rice cooker.</p>" :children []}]}
  {:name "Daniel Szmulewicz" :created "2014-10-13 07:59" :body "<p>Nice! I have to admit that the most puzzling thing for me is the fact that you call these eggs sous-vide. Sous-vide means that you cook food sealed in a bag. Typically, you will cook them longer and with lower temperatures than usual. It is a  French word. As is oeufs à la coque, which is what any Frenchmen will call the yummy egg you cooked. Boiled eggs is not as sexy a name, admittedly.</p>" :children [ {:name "Stian Eikeland" :created "2014-10-13 08:07" :body "<p>Hehe, thanks, and very true, sous vide definitely means cooking 'under vacuum'. Googling 'sous vide eggs' it looks like the term is slowly being expanded to mean anything cooking in a temperature controlled water bath (because of ignorance?). And though not technically correct I guess you can argue that eggs kinda come with their own vacuum bag/shell.</p><p>I guess a better naming here could be \"sous-vide-style\" eggs, the Food Lab seem to use that term: <a href=\"http://www.seriouseats.com/2013/10/sous-vide-101-all-about-eggs.html\" rel=\"nofollow\">http://www.seriouseats.com/...</a></p>" :children []}]}
 ]
 "/2015/04/24/banana-piano/"
 [  {:name "Виктор" :created "2015-04-25 18:19" :body "<p>Hello! Could you show the wiring diagram of bananas? Is \"ground\" used in wiring diagram? Do you know the project \"MaKey MaKey\"?  Thanks!<br>P.S. Sorry for my English. This theme is very useful for me)</p>" :children [ {:name "Stian Eikeland" :created "2015-04-25 20:34" :body "<p>Hello!<br>Wiring is actually super simple, ground plane is connected to ground pin on the arduino. Each banana is connected directly to their own digital pin. Just wires, there are no other components. So: Alu foil &lt;--&gt; GND.. First banana &lt;--&gt; D2.</p><p>Yeah, someone told me about the makey makey after i posted this. Looks pretty interesting (and easy to use). I don't know if they use the same hack, they might be using proper capacitive touch hardware - some microcontrollers even have built in capacitive touch sensing.</p>" :children []} {:name "Stian Eikeland" :created "2015-04-26 12:54" :body "<p>Post updated with more information about components and wiring :)</p>" :children [ {:name "Виктор" :created "2015-04-26 15:54" :body "<p>Oh! Thanks!</p>" :children []}]}]}
  {:name "Erik Assum" :created "2015-04-26 08:28" :body "<p>Stian, this is just crazy cool! As a total arduino n00b, I'd really appreciate if you could specify exactly what you used to make this happen.</p><p>It's the perfect thing for @kidsakoder!</p>" :children [ {:name "Stian Eikeland" :created "2015-04-26 12:52" :body "<p>Thanks! :) Post updated with more info. Good luck :)</p><p>Also, have a look at <a href=\"http://sonic-pi.net\" rel=\"nofollow\">http://sonic-pi.net</a> - created by same guy as overtone. But more suited for educational purposes. I don't know how easy it's to add a serial connection there, but should be doable.</p>" :children []}]}
  {:name "Valentina Mariño Arboleda" :created "2017-05-03 08:04" :body "<p>Hello!<br>Thank you so much for sharing!<br>Was wondering what exact kind of Capacitive TOuch sensor are you using?<br>I reckon this code  only works with a specific model of sensor?</p><p>Thank you so much :)</p>" :children []}
  {:name "Chau" :created "2025-12-01 00:14" :body "<p>Nine years later, and the information is still relevant. Thank you for your post. My friend and I are college students from the USA, and we used a Raspberry Pi 2 microcontroller with the built-in capacitive touch sensing. At first, I thought we were trying to close a circuit the traditional way, but since we didn't have a ground wire, I was confused, so I looked up more information to learn more. Now I know that the sensor in the Raspberry Pi is detecting the increase in the banana's capacitance. Thank you for your nice post, Mr. Eikeland.</p>" :children []}
